 "raw": {
  "prompt": "Given this presentation, what history, physical examination, and bedside tests should I perform? Include any diagnostic performance data you know.",
  "text": "Given this presentation\u2014an elderly woman with acute confusion, mild fever, and mild hypoxia, awaiting hip fracture surgery\u2014the most likely concern is delirium, possibly precipitated by infection, hypoxia, pain, or medication effects. Here\u2019s how to approach her:\n\n**History:**\n- Onset and course of confusion (acute vs. chronic)\n- Baseline cognitive function (from daughter)\n- Recent medications (especially sedatives, anticholinergics, opioids)\n- Pain assessment\n- Urinary symptoms (infection)\n- Respiratory symptoms (cough, dyspnea)\n- Bowel function (constipation)\n- Fluid intake and nutrition\n- Recent falls or trauma\n\n**Physical Examination:**\n- General: Level of consciousness, agitation, distress\n- Vital signs: Confirm temperature, HR, BP, RR, O2 sats\n- Respiratory: Auscultate for crackles, wheeze, signs of pneumonia\n- Cardiovascular: Rhythm, murmurs, perfusion\n- Abdomen: Tenderness, distension (urinary retention, constipation)\n- Neurological: Focal deficits, asterixis, tremor, nuchal rigidity\n- Skin: Pressure sores, signs of infection\n\n**Bedside Tests:**\n- Confusion Assessment Method (CAM):\n  - Sensitivity ~94%, specificity ~89% for delirium diagnosis\n  - Features: acute onset/fluctuating course, inattention, disorganized thinking, altered consciousness\n- 4AT (rapid delirium screen):\n  - Sensitivity ~89.7%, specificity ~84.1%\n- Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) for level of consciousness\n- Pain scale (e.g., Numeric Rating Scale or PAINAD for dementia)\n- Pulse oximetry (already low at 94%)\n- Urinalysis (bedside dipstick for infection)\n- Blood glucose (rule out hypoglycemia)\n\n**Summary:**\n- Acute delirium is likely; screen with CAM or 4AT.\n- Assess for infection (chest, urine), hypoxia, pain, medication effects, and metabolic derangements.\n- Involve daughter for collateral history.\n\n**References:**\n- Inouye SK et al. Ann Intern Med. 1990;113(12):941-8. (CAM)\n- Shenkin SD et al. Age Ageing. 2019;48(4):664-671. (4AT)\n- NICE Delirium Guidelines (CG103) 2010."
